Certainly! Here's a simple example of a responsive ad layout utilizing adaptive image handling within HTML:

```html
<div class="ad-container" data-ad-id="12345" data-campaign="summer-sale">
 <style>
 .ad-container {
 width: 100%;
 max-width: 300px;
 margin: 0 auto;
 text-align: center;
 border: 1px solid #ddd;
 padding: 10px;
 box-shadow: 0 0 5px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
 }
 .ad-image {
 width: 100%;
 height: auto;
 }
 .ad-content {
 font-size: 14px;
 color: #333;
 }
 .ad-cta {
 display: inline-block;
 margin-top: 10px;
 padding: 10px 15px;
 background-color: #28a745;
 color: #fff;
 text-decoration: none;
 border-radius: 5px;
 transition: background-color 0.3s;
 }
 .ad-cta:hover {
 background-color: #218838;
 }
 </style>
 <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/300x150" alt="Ad Banner" class="ad-image">
 <div class="ad-content">
 <h3>Special Offer!</h3>
 <p>Get 50% off on all items during our summer sale!</p>
 </div>
 <a href="#" class="ad-cta">Shop Now</a>
</div>
```
Classification: is_ad: true
Ad Type: banner

This HTML snippet represents a responsive ad layout using a flexible image that adapts to the container's width. It includes a call to action (CTA) to encourage user interaction. Notice the use of data attributes for tracking purposes.
